-- STEP 3: LESSON 1 - Introduction & Setup
-- =====================================================

INSERT INTO ai_learning_lessons (
  id,
  learning_path_id,
  title,
  description,
  content_type,
  content,
  duration_minutes,
  order_index,
  learning_objectives
) VALUES (
  gen_random_uuid(),
  'f8a9b0c1-d2e3-4f5a-6b7c-8d9e0f1a2b3c',
  'Introduction to Playwright with TypeScript',
  'Learn what Playwright is, how to install it, and understand browser automation architecture',
  'article',
  $$# Introduction to Playwright with TypeScript

## What is Playwright?

Think of Playwright as your **robot assistant that controls web browsers**. It can:
- Open websites automatically
- Click buttons and fill forms
- Check if things appear correctly
- Take screenshots and videos
- Test your web app on different browsers

**Real-world analogy:**
- Manual testing = You clicking through your website yourself
- Playwright = Training a robot to click through it for you, 100 times faster

## Why Use Playwright?

✅ **Save time** - Run tests in seconds instead of hours
✅ **Catch bugs early** - Find issues before users do
✅ **Test on multiple browsers** - Chrome, Firefox, Safari automatically
✅ **Reliable** - No more "flaky" tests that randomly fail
✅ **Built for modern web** - Works with React, Vue, Next.js, etc.

## Installation & Setup

:::playwright-practice language=typescript | height=400px | title=Your First Playwright Test | testUrl=https://playwright.dev
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('basic test example', async ({ page }) => {
  // Navigate to Playwright website
  await page.goto('https://playwright.dev');

  // Check page title
  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Playwright/);

  console.log('✅ Test passed!');
});
:::

## Understanding Playwright Architecture

Playwright has a simple 3-level structure:

### 1. Browser (Top Level)
The whole browser application (Chrome, Firefox, Safari)

### 2. Context (Middle Level)
A private browsing session (like incognito mode). Each context is isolated.

### 3. Page (Bottom Level)
A single tab within a context. This is where you do most of your work!

**Diagram:**
```
Browser
  ├── Context 1 (isolated session)
  │     ├── Page 1 (tab)
  │     └── Page 2 (tab)
  └── Context 2 (different isolated session)
        └── Page 1 (tab)
```

:::tip Key Point
Most tests use the `page` fixture which Playwright gives you automatically. You don't need to create browser/context manually!
:::

## Configuration Basics

The `playwright.config.ts` file controls how tests run:

```typescript
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  testDir: './tests',           // Where your tests are
  timeout: 30000,               // 30 seconds max per test
  retries: 2,                   // Retry failed tests twice
  use: {
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:3000',  // Your app URL
    screenshot: 'only-on-failure',     // Take screenshots when tests fail
    video: 'retain-on-failure',        // Record video of failures
  },
  projects: [
    { name: 'chromium', use: { browserName: 'chromium' } },
    { name: 'firefox', use: { browserName: 'firefox' } },
    { name: 'webkit', use: { browserName: 'webkit' } },
  ],
});
```

:::playwright-practice language=typescript | height=400px | title=Test Playwright Configuration | testUrl=https://example.com
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('verify configuration works', async ({ page }) => {
  // This test will run with default config
  await page.goto('https://example.com');

  // Verify page loaded
  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Example Domain/);

  // Find the heading
  const heading = page.getByRole('heading');
  await expect(heading).toBeVisible();

  console.log('✅ Configuration working!');
});
:::

## Running Tests

**Command line basics:**
```bash
# Run all tests
npx playwright test

# Run specific test file
npx playwright test tests/login.spec.ts

# Run in headed mode (see the browser)
npx playwright test --headed

# Run in debug mode (step through slowly)
npx playwright test --debug

# Generate HTML report
npx playwright show-report
```

:::warning Common Beginner Mistake
Tests run in **headless mode** by default (no visible browser). Use `--headed` flag to see what's happening while learning!
:::

## TypeScript Benefits

Playwright works great with TypeScript:

```typescript
import { test, expect, Page } from '@playwright/test';

// TypeScript knows what 'page' can do
test('typed example', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://example.com');

  // Autocomplete works! Your editor suggests methods
  await page.click('button');
  await page.fill('input[name="email"]', 'test@example.com');

  // Type-safe assertions
  await expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Welcome');
});
```

## Summary

**What you learned:**
- ✅ Playwright automates browser testing
- ✅ Installation: `npm init playwright@latest`
- ✅ Architecture: Browser → Context → Page
- ✅ Configuration in `playwright.config.ts`
- ✅ Run tests with `npx playwright test`
- ✅ TypeScript provides autocomplete and type safety

**Next lesson:** Write your first real test and learn core concepts!
$$,
  35,
  1,
  ARRAY[
    'Understand what Playwright is and its benefits',
    'Install and configure Playwright with TypeScript',
    'Understand browser automation architecture',
    'Run tests using command line options'
  ]
);

